INT. MERCY GENERAL - NURSES' STATION - NIGHT

3 a.m. The hospital hums. Vending machine glows. A mop bucket sits abandoned. Rain on the windows.

Its heritage week, so the whole floor is in costume. A banner reads "MERCY GENERAL: 100 YEARS OF CARING."

INES, 50s, night nurse, wears a starched 1920s nurse's cap and a cardigan. She counts pills. Dry as toast.

WALLY, 30s, resident, bursts through the doors in a full Victorian surgeon's apron and top hat.

(Applause beat.)

WALLY
Good evening Ines. Did you miss me?

INES
(not looking up)
Seven hours isn't long enough.

ANGLE ON Wally, who tips the hat. It falls off.

WALLY
It was a very sturdy hat this morning.

INES
They gave you the one with the moth.

He picks it up. Looks inside. A moth flutters out.

WALLY
Well. It's alive. That's more than I can say for my evening.

She slides a chart across the counter without a word.

INSERT - THE CHART. Room 9. Patient: M. Alder. Allergies: none. Next of kin: blank.

BACK TO SCENE

WALLY
Whos Mr. Alder?

INES
Brought in at midnight. No visitors. No calls.

WALLY
Maybe he's shy.

INES
Maybe.

She sorts the pills again. Same pills. Same order.

WALLY
You've counted those three times.

INES
Four.

He looks at her. She doesn't look back. The vending machine HUMS.

WALLY
Ines. Is something

INES
Pass me the pen.

He passes the pen. She writes "Alder" and then crosses it out and writes it again, slower.

WE SEE through the glass of Room 9, a man's hand resting on the blanket, a wedding ring catching the light.

WALLY
(softly)
You know him.

INES
I know a lot of people.

WALLY
Ines.

INES
He was a medic. In the old days. We worked a flood together in '09. He carried me out of a basement.

She puts the pen down very carefully, like it might break.

WALLY
Shall I

INES
No. I'll go.

She straightens her cap. Walks to Room 9. At the door she pauses, one hand on the frame, and for a second the whole floor is silent. Even the rain.

CLOSE ON Wally, who takes off his ridiculous hat and holds it against his chest.

She goes in. The door closes.

The monitor beeps once. Then again, faster.
